A||||3||Uaana||Yet another tire question||||1||
Z||000000||Uaana||11-22-2000||05:00 PM||||So what's the widest tire you can fit on a 15x6 rim IE sunfire/grand am rim.. <BR>Found a nice set and am wondering if I can keep with the stock 215/60/15 or if I need to use something smaller like a 205/195||||reg||1
Z||000001||Feenx||11-22-2000||09:30 PM||||you can put 215/60/15 on a 6" rim....<BR>but you will find that the tread will round over and look funny<BR>the first three numbers of a tire size refer to the hight of the tire not how wide it is.<BR>I would sujest using 215/65/15 or even 205/70/15's<BR>||||reg||9
Z||000002||N_Man||11-23-2000||02:39 AM||||Feenx<BR>The first three numbers of a tire DO refer to how wide it is.  Followed by the next two that refer to the height of the side wall and the last two the fim size.<BR>205(mm wide)/60(mm high)/15(inch wheel)<P>Uaana<BR>215's would be VERY wide for a 6 inch wheel.  It will depend on the particular tire though.  <BR>A narrower tire to fit the rim properly will handle BETTER than the widest tire you can stuff on it.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Oreif||11-23-2000||10:47 AM||||First to read the numbers on a tire are <BR>205 (mm wide) 60 (aspect ratio of side wall to tread width in percentage)(Example a 225/50r15 has a sidewall 4.4" tall and a 235/50r15 has a 4.7" side wall) /15 (wheel size in inches) <BR>A 215 size tire will fit just fine on a 6" wide wheel. That is the widest you can go without over loading the sidewall of the tire. You can go to 235 wide but the sidewalls will not be supported properly and handling will suffer. The ideal tire width is to have the tire less that 1" past the wheel. So a 6" wheel should have a tire no wider than 8" wide. You can go up to an 1.25" on either side and properly support the tire (a 8.5" wide tire) but anything wider isn't good functionally. <BR> Next you do not want to change the overall diameter of the tire too much this will affect the speedometer readings. My car originally had 215/60R14's (on 14X6" wheels) and I went to 225/50R15's (on 15x7" wheels). This gave me a wider tire but the overall diameter is really close. My speedo is less than 1% difference from original. This link will take you to a "Tire Calculator" Put in your original tire size and the new tire size. It will than give you most measurements in inches and give you the percentage of speedo error.<BR> <A HREF="http://www.secret-secret.com/turbo/DIY/tirecalc/turbotire.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.secret-secret.com/turbo/DIY/tirecalc/turbotire.html</A> <P>You may end up going to a 50 series tire to maintain the overall diameter when going to a 15" wheel. Most Fiero's (notchback styles except 88 Formula)) had the 14X6 wheels as largest stock wheels. So 15" = 50 series, 16" = 40 series. GT's and Formulas started at 15" 60 series tire so they can go up to 17" and 40 series without recalibrating the speedo.<BR>Hope this helps.<BR>||||reg||1
